    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-2">Reading notes</div>
    <h1>A reading list on modal meaning</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-8">
      The lineage behind the essay, ordered so each stage equips the next. A personal study list, not a
      survey — I keep it here rather than in the essay.
    </p>

    <h2>Modal logic — the formal layer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Hughes &amp; Cresswell, <em>A New Introduction to Modal Logic</em>: chapters 1–3 for working fluency (K, T, S4, S5; Kripke frames; axiom-to-frame correspondence).</li>
      <li>Blackburn, de Rijke &amp; Venema, <em>Modal Logic</em> (ch. 1) for depth; the SEP entry "Modal Logic" as a map.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Meaning as structure — the linguistic layer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Kratzer, "The Notional Category of Modality" (1981; revised in <em>Modals and Conditionals</em>, 2012): modal base, ordering source, the thin semantics of modal words.</li>
      <li>von Fintel &amp; Gillies, "Must … Stay … Strong!" (<em>NALS</em> 2010): why epistemic <em>must</em> is weaker than a bare assertion, and how a worlds account survives it.</li>
      <li>Lassiter, <em>Graded Modality</em> (2017), and von Fintel &amp; Heim, <em>Intensional Semantics</em>: the degree camp, and a rigorous entry to the worlds camp.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Meaning in the mind — the developmental layer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Leahy &amp; Carey, "The Acquisition of Modal Concepts," <em>TiCS</em> 2020: the minimal/modal distinction and the task logic built on it.</li>
      <li>Cesana-Arlotti et&nbsp;al. and the developmental literature on early logical/modal reasoning: what a possession-versus-performance test looks like in practice.</li>
      <li>Quilty-Dunn, Porot &amp; Mandelbaum, "The Best Game in Town," <em>BBS</em> 2023: the representational-format debate in its current form.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The question posed to language models — the experimental layer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Linzen, "How Can We Accelerate Progress Towards Human-like Linguistic Generalization?" (ACL 2020): the evaluation philosophy this line adopts.</li>
      <li>McCoy, Pavlick &amp; Linzen, "Right for the Wrong Reasons" (ACL 2019): surface heuristics vs. licensing structure.</li>
      <li>Recent work on modal and epistemic reasoning in language models, tracked as the literature develops.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Historical companions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Leibniz, "On Freedom and Possibility" and the Arnauld correspondence: possible worlds in their original habitat.</li>
      <li>Montague, "The Proper Treatment of Quantification in Ordinary English" (1973): natural language as a formal language.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="!mt-10 !text-sm"><a href="/writing/logic-of-natural-language" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Back to the essay</a></p>
  </article>
